createKey	2026-09-07	RSA-HSM and EC-HSM are REFUSED with 400, not normalised to software keys. Quietly downgrading them let a caller verify 'our keys are HSM-backed' and get a passing run plus a false belief. A Go test used to assert the downgrade was correct, so the test encoded the bug: if you make HSM 'work', check what the test is really claiming.	2c5f2f9d87a5f0852ff7d6ac9d0bb30b8e48ee1d	main
linkURL	2026-09-07	do NOT collapse this into baseURL. baseURL is the vault's canonical identity and must stay {vault}.vault.azure.net for id/kid/sid/recoveryId, which is what the SDKs expect. linkURL is the host the caller actually dialled, and nextLink must use it or every SDK pager dies on page two against localhost. Our own tests parsed nextLink instead of following it, which is how that shipped broken through four releases.	2c5f2f9d87a5f0852ff7d6ac9d0bb30b8e48ee1d	main
portal/embed.go	2026-09-07	committed and go:embed'ed on purpose, so 'go install @latest' needs no Node. It is content-hashed, so ANY svelte/vite bump changes the filename and CI's 'Portal builds from source' gate fails. Dependabot cannot rebuild it, so every such PR lands red and needs a rebuild commit. Reproduce the way CI does (wipe node_modules and portal/dist, pnpm install --frozen-lockfile, rebuild) because a grouped install resolves transitives differently and yields a different hash for the same versions. Run 'smoke', not just 'test': a svelte bump can build and pass jsdom while resolving Svelte's server build and never mounting.	2c5f2f9d87a5f0852ff7d6ac9d0bb30b8e48ee1d	main
secretWindowRefusal	2026-09-07	nbf/exp refusal is 403, never 404. Azure distinguishes 'not yours yet' from 'no such secret' and callers depend on it. Keys deliberately differ: there only cryptographic ops are refused while metadata reads stay permissive, because a key's public half is not the secret. That asymmetry is Azure's, not ours.	2c5f2f9d87a5f0852ff7d6ac9d0bb30b8e48ee1d	main
